    { math: '1 · The materials genome idea', note: 'Compute properties from first principles (quantum mechanics — density functional theory, Nobel 1998) instead of synthesising every candidate: screen millions of compositions in silico, make only the winners. The Materials Genome Initiative (2011) industrialised the pipeline.' },
    { math: '2 · DFT: the workhorse', note: 'Solving the electron density (not the full wavefunction — the mathematical shortcut that made it tractable) predicts lattice constants, formation energies, band gaps and elastic constants from the periodic table alone. Accurate enough to rank candidates, fast enough to screen them.' },
    { math: '3 · Databases + ML: the acceleration', note: 'DFT-filled databases (Materials Project: 150,000+ computed compounds) train machine-learning models that predict properties in milliseconds instead of CPU-days (the AI lesson\'s surrogate models) — the screening frontier went from thousands to billions of candidates.' },
    { math: '4 · The autonomous lab closes the loop', note: 'Robot synthesis + characterisation + ML choosing the next experiment (self-driving laboratories): the design–make–test cycle shrinking from years to days. Batteries, catalysts and alloys arriving via the pipeline — materials discovery learning the robotics lesson\'s automation grammar.' },
  ],
