<TopicPage slug="subjects/materials-science/expert/computational-materials-design">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Computational materials design is the field\'s culmination: <strong>discovery as a pipeline
    — predict with quantum mechanics, accelerate with machine learning, verify with autonomous
    experiments</strong>. The serendipity era (penicillin, Teflon, most alloys) is giving way to
    engineered search.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the genome idea, DFT, ML acceleration, the autonomous loop" widget="equationStep" config={compmatCfg} />

  <h2>What the pipeline delivers</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>High-entropy alloys:</strong> five-plus elements in near-equal parts — the design space was computationally invisible before (millions of combinations); screening found exceptional strength–toughness and temperature combinations now entering applications.</li>
    <li><strong>Battery and catalyst discovery:</strong> voltage plateaus, ion mobility and catalytic activity computed before a single synthesis — the energy lesson\'s storage and the chemistry lesson\'s kinetics, pre-searched (solid-state electrolytes moved from obscure to industry-funded via this route).</li>
    <li><strong>The honest limits:</strong> DFT errors (band gaps famously under-predicted), ML\'s extrapolation problem (models are only as good as their training space — the evaluation lesson\'s distribution shift, in a flask), and synthesis reality: predicted compounds that refuse to form. The loop exists because prediction alone is never enough.</li>
    <li><strong>Where the ladder lands:</strong> structure→properties (the first lesson) became properties→structure: inverse design — specify the property, search for the material. The discipline that began identifying sand now designs substances that do not exist yet.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Kohn, Nobel lecture (1998); Jain et al., APL Materials (Materials Project, 2013); the AI ladder\'s ML lessons; de Pablo et al., Nature (2019) on the MGI.</p>
</TopicPage>
